    ],
    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Spain is famous for its long days. Lunch doesn’t start until two in the afternoon; work often finishes after seven in the evening; dinner begins at half past eight, at the "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b1",
            "number": 1
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Marta Junqué, of the Time Use Institute based in Barcelona, says this hasn’t always been the case. “My grandparents worked like everyone else,” she explains.“They got up at sunrise and stopped working when night fell. Now, at six or seven, we are still "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b2",
            "number": 2
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ".”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Still, Spaniards work only slightly more than the European average—37.8 hours a week—but they "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b3",
            "number": 3
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " less than people in many other European countries. The main reason for this is the country’s famous long afternoon break, called “the siesta”. It comes from the Latin word “sexta”, meaning the 6th hour after dawn. The siesta was a traditional "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b4",
            "number": 4
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " for "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "agricultural",
            "ref": "v-agricultural"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " workers when the heat was at its "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "peak",
            "ref": "v-peak"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The whole country got used to the siesta during World War Ⅱ when people had to take several jobs. “They would get up at "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "dawn",
            "ref": "v-dawn"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " to work for six to eight hours, take a break for two or three hours, and commute to another "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b5",
            "number": 5
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ",” Junqué says. “Then, they would work for several more hours into the evening.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "It’s a "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b6",
            "number": 6
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " that has stuck. Today, many shops still "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b7",
            "number": 7
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " for a two-or three-hour break, which is why employees spend more hours in total at work. The "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b8",
            "number": 8
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " is that Spain’s productivity levels are lower than those of many other European countries. “There’s plenty of proof that the longer you stay at work, the less productive you are,” Junqué says."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "For years, Spain has been trying to fix its internal clock. "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b9",
            "number": 9
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", the current government has required employers to pay extra to those who work between 10 p.m. and 6 a.m. the next morning. But that doesn’t really solve the problem. Late night is still a golden moment for Spanish people, and there are no "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b10",
            "number": 10
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " that this will change anytime soon."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
